.Dd September 1, 2026
.Dt SD-HEALTHCHECK 1
.Os
.
.Sh NAME
.Nm sd-healthcheck
.Nd translate a command health check into systemd readiness and watchdog notifications
.
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl -
.Ar command
.Op Ar argument ...
.Nm
.Fl h
.Nm
.Fl -help
.Nm
.Fl -version
.
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
starts a service as a child process and runs a separate, container-style
health-check command.
The first successful check completes systemd service startup by sending
.Dq READY=1 .
Later successful checks keep the systemd service watchdog alive by sending
.Dq WATCHDOG=1 .
.
.Pp
The service command is taken from the command-line arguments.
Health-check and systemd notification settings are read exclusively from the
environment.
Consequently, a
.Dq --
separator is not required, but an optional leading separator is accepted.
.
.Pp
The service unit must normally set
.Dq Type=notify
and
.Dq WatchdogSec= .
The health check uses
.Pa /bin/sh
.Fl c ,
matching the shell form of Docker's
.Ic HEALTHCHECK CMD .
An exit status of zero means healthy; any other result means unhealthy.
.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print usage information and exit.
.It Fl -version
Print the program version and exit.
.It Fl -
End option processing.
When present, this separator must precede the service command.
.El
.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev SD_HEALTHCHECK_CMD
Required shell command used to check the service.
The command is run using
.Pa /bin/sh
.Fl c .
Do not include secrets directly in this value; have the command read them from
a protected file or a systemd credential instead.
.It Ev NOTIFY_SOCKET
Required systemd notification socket, normally provided to a service using
.Dq Type=notify .
.It Ev WATCHDOG_USEC
Required watchdog timeout in microseconds, normally provided by
.Dq WatchdogSec= .
The value determines the probe interval, timeout, and retry interval.
.It Ev WATCHDOG_PID
Required process ID for the watchdog owner, normally provided by
.Dq WatchdogSec= .
It must identify the
.Nm
process.
.El
.
.Pp
.Nm
owns the systemd notification protocol.
It removes
.Ev NOTIFY_SOCKET ,
.Ev WATCHDOG_USEC ,
.Ev WATCHDOG_PID ,
all variables beginning with
.Ev SD_HEALTHCHECK_ ,
and systemd socket-activation variables from both the service and health-check
environments.
.
.Sh OPERATION
.Bl -enum
.It
The service is started in a new process group and the health check is run
immediately.
.It
Before the first successful check, failures are retried every one tenth of the
watchdog timeout.
The service remains in its systemd starting state, so
.Dq TimeoutStartSec=
controls the maximum startup time.
.It
The first successful check sends
.Dq READY=1 ,
.Dq WATCHDOG=1 ,
and a healthy status in one notification.
.It
After startup, checks normally run every half of the watchdog timeout.
Each check may run for at most one quarter of the watchdog timeout.
.It
A successful check sends a watchdog keep-alive notification.
A failed check reports an unhealthy status, withholds the watchdog keep-alive,
and retries after one tenth of the watchdog timeout.
If health does not recover, systemd applies its configured watchdog action.
.El
.
.Pp
There is no independent failure threshold or restart policy.
.Dq TimeoutStartSec= ,
.Dq WatchdogSec= ,
and
.Dq Restart=
in the service unit remain the sources of truth.
Watchdog timeouts below 100 milliseconds are rejected.
.
.Pp
Health-check standard output and standard error are streamed to the
corresponding
.Nm
streams.
Successful checks should normally remain quiet to avoid unnecessary journal
traffic.
.
.Sh CHECK DESIGN
The configured command serves two purposes: its first success declares service
startup complete, and a sustained later failure permits systemd to terminate or
restart the service.
The check should therefore test a condition for which both actions are safe.
.
.Pp
In particular, avoid treating temporary overload or failure of a remote
dependency as proof that the local service must be restarted.
Such checks can cause restart cascades.
If runtime traffic readiness must be withdrawn without restarting the service,
implement that check in the load balancer or service-discovery layer;
systemd's
.Dq READY=1
notification is a one-time startup transition.
.
.Sh SIGNALS
.Nm
forwards
.Dv SIGTERM ,
.Dv SIGINT ,
.Dv SIGQUIT ,
and
.Dv SIGABRT
to the service process group and waits for it to exit.
.Dv SIGHUP ,
.Dv SIGUSR1 ,
and
.Dv SIGUSR2
are passed through while supervision continues.
.
.Pp
Using
.Dq KillMode=mixed
is recommended.
It lets systemd signal
.Nm
first so it can forward the stop signal, while still allowing systemd to kill
all remaining processes in the unit if shutdown times out.
.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits with status zero when the service exits successfully or a coordinated
systemd stop completes.
A non-zero service exit status is preserved.
If the service terminates from an uncoordinated signal, the status is 128 plus
the signal number.
Configuration, startup, and notification errors produce status 1.
Health-check failures do not directly terminate
.Nm ;
they are handled through the systemd startup timeout or service watchdog.
.
.Sh LIMITATIONS
Socket-activated services are not supported.
If systemd supplies descriptors through
.Ev LISTEN_FDS ,
.Nm
exits before starting the service.
Use the service's native systemd watchdog integration instead.
.
.Pp
Runtime readiness cannot be withdrawn after
.Dq READY=1
has been sent.
The status reported by
.Nm
after startup is health information, not a Kubernetes-style traffic-routing
signal.
.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Run an HTTP service with a command health check:
.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[Service]
Type=notify
NotifyAccess=main
KillMode=mixed
WatchdogSec=30s
TimeoutStartSec=2min
Restart=on-failure

Environment="SD_HEALTHCHECK_CMD=/usr/bin/curl --fail --silent --show-error --max-time 2 http://127.0.0.1:3000/healthz"
ExecStart=/usr/bin/sd-healthcheck /usr/local/bin/example-server --port 3000
.Ed
.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr sd_notify 3 ,
.Xr systemd.kill 5 ,
.Xr systemd.service 5
.
